  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-704535b20e6b::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-704535b20e6b",
          "text": "Movies like “Limitless” and “Lucy” play on the notion that humans use only 10 percent of their brains—and those who unlock a higher percentage wield powers like infinite memory or telekinesis. ",
          "start": 145,
          "end": 338,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-704535b20e6b::sentence-2",
              "documentId": "doc-704535b20e6b",
              "text": "The human brain houses incredibly complex and advanced functions—but do we only access a fraction of it?\n",
              "start": 40,
              "end": 145,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-704535b20e6b::sentence-4",
              "documentId": "doc-704535b20e6b",
              "text": "It’s enticing to think that so much of the brain remains untapped and is ripe for boosting human potential.\n",
              "start": 338,
              "end": 446,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "The human brain houses incredibly complex and advanced functions—but do we only access a fraction of it? Movies like “Limitless” and “Lucy” play on the notion that humans use only 10 percent of their brains—and those who unlock a higher percentage wield powers like infinite memory or telekinesis. It’s enticing to think that so much of the brain remains untapped and is ripe for boosting human potential."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-704535b20e6b::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-704535b20e6b",
          "text": "Do we only use 10 percent of our brain?\n",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 40,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "Do we only use 10 percent of our brain?"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-704535b20e6b::sentence-5",
          "documentId": "doc-704535b20e6b",
          "text": "But the idea that we use 10 percent of our brain is 100 percent a myth.\n",
          "start": 446,
          "end": 518,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-704535b20e6b::sentence-4",
              "documentId": "doc-704535b20e6b",
              "text": "It’s enticing to think that so much of the brain remains untapped and is ripe for boosting human potential.\n",
              "start": 338,
              "end": 446,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "It’s enticing to think that so much of the brain remains untapped and is ripe for boosting human potential. But the idea that we use 10 percent of our brain is 100 percent a myth."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-2",
          "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
          "text": "This time, the movie myth that needs busting is that human beings use only 10 percent of their brains—a \"fact\" Freeman cites while playing Professor Norman in the TV ads for Lucy, whose title character (played by Scarlett Johansson) \"is able to kick butt and take names … because some drug made her a super-powered brainiac.\"\n",
          "start": 167,
          "end": 493,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-1",
              "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
              "text": "in The Lego Movie, The Washington Post points out Freeman's latest character is once again doling out Hollywood falsehoods with authority in the summer thriller Lucy.\n",
              "start": 0,
              "end": 167,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-3",
              "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
              "text": "But it's pure science fiction that it takes a chemical cocktail to make humans use their entire brains. ",
              "start": 493,
              "end": 597,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "in The Lego Movie, The Washington Post points out Freeman's latest character is once again doling out Hollywood falsehoods with authority in the summer thriller Lucy. This time, the movie myth that needs busting is that human beings use only 10 percent of their brains—a \"fact\" Freeman cites while playing Professor Norman in the TV ads for Lucy, whose title character (played by Scarlett Johansson) \"is able to kick butt and take names … because some drug made her a super-powered brainiac.\" But it's pure science fiction that it takes a chemical cocktail to make humans use their entire brains."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
          "text": "But it's pure science fiction that it takes a chemical cocktail to make humans use their entire brains. ",
          "start": 493,
          "end": 597,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-2",
              "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
              "text": "This time, the movie myth that needs busting is that human beings use only 10 percent of their brains—a \"fact\" Freeman cites while playing Professor Norman in the TV ads for Lucy, whose title character (played by Scarlett Johansson) \"is able to kick butt and take names … because some drug made her a super-powered brainiac.\"\n",
              "start": 167,
              "end": 493,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-4",
              "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
              "text": "It turns out that we are all already giving it everything we've got, according to Dr. Barry Gordon, a professor of neurology at the School of Medicine and professor of cognitive science at the Krieger School of Arts and Sciences.\n",
              "start": 597,
              "end": 827,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "This time, the movie myth that needs busting is that human beings use only 10 percent of their brains—a \"fact\" Freeman cites while playing Professor Norman in the TV ads for Lucy, whose title character (played by Scarlett Johansson) \"is able to kick butt and take names … because some drug made her a super-powered brainiac.\" But it's pure science fiction that it takes a chemical cocktail to make humans use their entire brains. It turns out that we are all already giving it everything we've got, according to Dr. Barry Gordon, a professor of neurology at the School of Medicine and professor of cognitive science at the Krieger School of Arts and Sciences."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-6",
          "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
          "text": "Gordon, a behavioral neurologist and cognitive neuroscientist, told Scientific American that \"we use virtually every part of the brain, and that [most of] the brain is active almost all the time,\" he said. ",
          "start": 918,
          "end": 1124,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd::sentence-5",
              "documentId": "doc-bf1bf358b8bd",
              "text": "The Post refers to an interview with Dr. Gordon, published by Scientific American in 2008.\n",
              "start": 827,
              "end": 918,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "The Post refers to an interview with Dr. Gordon, published by Scientific American in 2008. Gordon, a behavioral neurologist and cognitive neuroscientist, told Scientific American that \"we use virtually every part of the brain, and that [most of] the brain is active almost all the time,\" he said."
        }
